How about this:
1. Zapping a ray of cold at something would create an ice block
containing that object (or all objects on that spot).
2. If the object is a monster, then it is trapped inside for a random
number of turns, unless the monster is large size or bigger.  Cold
resistant monsters are trapped for a shorter period of time.  Strong
monsters will break out after one turn.
3. Encased corpses and food age exactly as if it were in an icebox,
that is to say, not at all.  This is balanced by the fact that blocks
of ice resemble, and behave in many ways, exactly like boulders, so
unless you're a giant, they ain't portable.  Of course you could always
BE a giant, but then you've got other problems.
4. Ice can be melted by zapping it with a fire ray (in whatever method
of that you have), revealing the items inside.  Such items are
waterlogged, so they are not succeptable to burning, but they are
succeptable to water damage (potion dilution, scroll blanking, metal
rusting).  It also washes out some types of engravings, dust and burnt
among them.  You can also get the stuff out with a pick-axe, of course,
or by any other means of destroying boulders.
5. Blocks of ice behave much like boulders, except if you push one, it
slides from one to four spaces.  If it's resting on ice, it'll continue
to slide until it hits a wall or runs out of ice.  (This would make
devious new kinds of Sokoban puzzles possible....)  An ice boulder that
contains a monster does not produce enough water, when it's melted, to
fill a pit, and the ice will break as soon as the block lands in the
pit to prevent having to store monsters in ice floor.  Monsters could
potentially be drowned by zapping with a wand of ice, then sliding the
block into a moat larger than one square in size: the ice
shatters/melts upon entering the pool, the monster is dropped into the
water, then if it is unable to escape it drowns.  (There is game
precident for this, as anyone who's lost a pet to an unlucky fountain
quaff can attest.)
6. A block of ice that slides into a pit becomes a square of ice floor,
and if it's then melted it becomes a pool of water.  This means, with a
charged wand of cold, a generic item, and a pick-axe, you can make
reliable pools of water at a cost of one pool per charge.

How's that?

